# November 22, 2026: Missouri, a first scatter across the state. Missouri had 23 places, nearly all in Kansas City.
# This batch spreads 18 places over the rest of the state: the Ozarks, the Old Lead Belt, the Bootheel, the
# Mississippi river towns, the German Missouri River valley and the north-west. Facts from the linked sources only:
# Wikipedia read as raw text, Missouri State Parks pages (each site's page and its hours page), National Park
# Service place pages, and the pages of the museums that run the private sites. Pins checked on Esri satellite.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
MSP='https://mostateparks.com/'
PLACES=[
N('ha-ha-tonka-castle-ruins','Ha Ha Tonka castle ruins','Camdenton','kcmo','landmark','ruins',37.97609,-92.7698,'exact','public','walk','burned','1905–1942',
  'The Kansas City businessman Robert McClure Snyder began a stone mansion here in 1905, modelled on European castles, above a spring and the Niangua valley. He died in a car accident in 1906 and his sons finished it in the early 1920s; the family used it as a summer and weekend house. It was run as a hotel in the late 1930s and burned in 1942. The state bought it in 1978 and it is now the centrepiece of Ha Ha Tonka State Park.',
  'The roofless stone walls of the house on the bluff, and the separate stone water tower, which was repaired with a new roof in 2004. The castle walls have been stabilised. Trails and boardwalks lead from the ruins down to the spring, sinkholes and a natural bridge.',
  'A burned millionaire\'s castle on a bluff over the Lake of the Ozarks.',
  'Ha Ha Tonka State Park (Missouri State Parks), 1491 State Road D, south of Camdenton. Parking on Castle Ruins Road near the ruins. Grounds open 7 a.m. to sunset April to October and 8 a.m. to sunset November to March; the gates close at sunset. Hours from the park\'s own hours page.',
  [W('Ha Ha Tonka State Park'),['Missouri State Parks: Ha Ha Tonka',MSP+'park/ha-ha-tonka-state-park'],['Park hours',MSP+'park/ha-ha-tonka-state-park/hours']],
  th=[37.97822,-92.76543],thn='Castle Ruins Road lot',brick='?',hours='Grounds 7 a.m. to sunset April to October, 8 a.m. to sunset November to March'),
N('maramec-iron-works','Maramec Iron Works','St. James','kcmo','industry','ruins',37.955833,-91.538611,'site','open','walk','','1826–1876',
  'The first iron works west of the Mississippi to pay its way made iron here from 1826 to 1876, using the flow of Maramec Spring to drive its machinery and smelting high-grade hematite from a pit nearby. In the Civil War it made iron for cannonballs and for James B. Eads\'s gunboats, built in St. Louis. The spring and about 1,800 acres round it belong to the James Foundation, given by Lucy Wortham James, and are kept as a public park.',
  'The ruins of the iron works stand beside the spring, among them the remains of a furnace. A park drive passes a cemetery of iron workers, the iron pit mine and old homestead sites, and a museum run by the foundation tells the story of the spring and the works. The Missouri Department of Conservation runs a trout hatchery at the spring.',
  'The first paying iron works west of the Mississippi, beside a spring that still pours out about 100 million gallons a day.',
  'Maramec Spring Park, near St. James, owned and run by the James Foundation as a public park. The foundation\'s own website refused to be read when this was written, so its hours and entrance fee are not checked: look at its page before going. Mapped parking along Maramec Spring Drive and the Pumphouse Trail.',
  [W('Maramec Spring'),['Maramec Spring Park','https://maramecspringpark.com/about']],
  th=[37.95637,-91.53634],thn='Pumphouse Trail lot',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('alley-spring-mill','Alley Spring Mill (Red Mill)','Eminence','kcmo','industry','standing',37.15419,-91.44201,'exact','open','walk','','1893–',
  'A roller grist mill built at Alley Spring in the 1890s: Wikipedia and its Register listing give 1893, the National Park Service place page 1894. It is a two-and-a-half-storey frame building on limestone blocks, 32 by 42 feet, and holds four steel roller mills and one millstone. The spring that drove it is one of the largest in Missouri and stays at 57 degrees all year. It was listed on the National Register in 1981 and was pictured on a 2017 quarter.',
  'The red frame mill beside the deep blue spring pool, with its roller machinery inside. Interior not checked beyond the machinery the sources name.',
  'One of the most photographed mills anywhere, on a spring pool of strange deep blue.',
  'Ozark National Scenic Riverways (National Park Service), on Route 106 west of Eminence; parking by the Scenic Overlook Trail beside the mill. The mill has opened to visitors in the warm season (a 2013 park release gave daily opening from Memorial Day, 9 a.m. to 4:45 p.m.), but no current hours could be read when this was written: hours not checked.',
  [W('Alley Spring Roller Mill'),['NPS: Alley Spring and Mill','https://www.nps.gov/places/alley-spring-and-mill.htm'],['NPS release, 2013','https://www.nps.gov/ozar/learn/news/ozark-national-scenic-riverways-announces-spring-facilities-open.htm']],
  th=[37.15377,-91.44282],thn='Scenic Overlook Trail lot',brick='no',hours='?',conf='mixed'),
N('dillard-mill','Dillard Mill','Davisville','kcmo','industry','standing',37.71748,-91.20682,'exact','open','walk','','1908–1956',
  'Francis Wisdom built a mill on Huzzah Creek in 1853; it burned in 1895. A new mill, the Mische Mill, went up in 1908 and ran on an underwater turbine instead of a wheel, its owners changing the course of the creek and cutting the bluff to suit it. It ground until 1956. The state has run the site since 1975 under a lease from the L-A-D Foundation, which owns it, and restoration was finished in 1980.',
  'A red mill on the creek with most of its machinery intact and original to the building, according to Missouri State Parks. Guided tours go through it; between tours the first floor can be seen with staff.',
  'A red mill on blue Ozark water, its machinery still in place.',
  'Dillard Mill State Historic Site, 142 Dillard Mill Road, Davisville: owned by the L-A-D Foundation and run by Missouri State Parks. Parking off Dillard Mill Road about 350 m north of the mill, with the Mill View Trail. Grounds 8 a.m. to 8 p.m. May to October and 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. November to April. Hours from the site\'s own hours page.',
  [W('Dillard Mill State Historic Site'),['Missouri State Parks: Dillard Mill',MSP+'historic-site/dillard-mill-state-historic-site'],['Site hours',MSP+'hours/791/historic-site/dillard-mill-state-historic-site']],
  th=[37.72013,-91.20447],thn='Mill View Trail lot',brick='?',hours='Tours 10 a.m., 12:30 and 2:30 p.m., Wednesday to Sunday May to October, Friday to Sunday November to April'),
N('bollinger-mill-burfordville-bridge','Bollinger Mill and Burfordville Covered Bridge','Burfordville','kcmo','industry','standing',37.36815,-89.8027,'exact','open','walk','','1825–1953',
  'George Frederick Bollinger, who came from North Carolina with a Spanish land grant, began a log dam and mill on the Whitewater River in 1800 and rebuilt them in limestone in 1825. The Union army burned the mill in the Civil War. Solomon Burford finished the present four-storey brick mill in 1867 on the 1825 stone foundation, and it ground until 1953. The covered bridge beside it was begun in 1858, held up by the war and finished in 1868, which makes it the oldest of Missouri\'s four surviving covered bridges.',
  'The four-storey brick mill on its stone base, with the dam, and the covered bridge: a Howe truss of local yellow poplar, 140 feet long, which had a toll booth at its east end until 1906. Mill tours climb three flights of steep open stairs; the mill is not heated or cooled and has wasps. Visitors can walk through the bridge.',
  'A Civil War-burned mill rebuilt in brick, beside Missouri\'s oldest covered bridge.',
  'Bollinger Mill State Historic Site (Missouri State Parks), Burfordville, Cape Girardeau County. Parking in the gravel lot off Bollinger Mill Road just south of the mill. Grounds sunrise to sunset all year. Tours last about 45 minutes, ten people at most, the last at 3 p.m. Hours from the site\'s own hours page.',
  [W('Bollinger Mill State Historic Site'),['Missouri State Parks: Bollinger Mill',MSP+'historic-site/bollinger-mill-state-historic-site'],['Site hours',MSP+'hours/866/historic-site/bollinger-mill-state-historic-site']],
  th=[37.36703,-89.80253],thn='Gravel lot south of the mill',brick='yes',hours='Mill tours 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. Tuesday to Saturday and noon to 4 p.m. Sunday, April to October; Thursday to Sunday, November to March'),
N('watkins-woolen-mill','Watkins Woolen Mill','Lawson','kcmo','industry','standing',39.41291,-94.25926,'exact','open','walk','','1859–1886',
  'Waltus Watkins set up a livestock farm, Bethany Plantation, in 1839, and built the woolen mill in 1859 and 1860, with houses for his workers. The mill ran at full capacity until 1886, two years after his death, and declined after that. Nearly all its machinery survives, with the 65-horsepower steam engine that drove it, and it was made a National Historic Landmark in 1966. Missouri State Parks calls it the only 19th-century textile mill in the United States with its original machinery still intact.',
  'The three-storey mill with its machinery, the 12-room Watkins house of 1850, an octagonal brick schoolhouse of about 1856 (the Franklin School) and the Mount Vernon Baptist Church of 1871. A visitor centre tells the family\'s story.',
  'A woolen mill whose machinery was simply left where it stood.',
  'Watkins Woolen Mill State Historic Site, 26600 Park Road North, Lawson, inside Watkins Mill State Park (Missouri State Parks). Parking on Watkins Road south of the mill. For tour times the site asks visitors to telephone. Hours from the site\'s own hours page.',
  [W('Watkins Woolen Mill State Park and State Historic Site'),['Missouri State Parks: Watkins Woolen Mill',MSP+'historic-site/watkins-woolen-mill-state-historic-site'],['Site hours',MSP+'hours/846/historic-site/watkins-woolen-mill-state-historic-site']],
  th=[39.41051,-94.25874],thn='Watkins Road lot',brick='yes',hours='Grounds and museum 9:30 a.m. to 5 p.m. Monday to Saturday, 10:30 a.m. to 5 p.m. Sunday (closing 4:30 November 1 to April 14); mill tours by telephone'),
N('fort-davidson-pilot-knob','Fort Davidson','Pilot Knob','kcmo','fort','traces',37.61948,-90.63994,'exact','public','walk','forts','1864',
  'Union soldiers built this six-sided earthen fort in the valley at Pilot Knob. On September 27, 1864, during Price\'s Raid, its garrison beat off Confederate attacks under Maj. Gen. Sterling Price, who led about 8,000 men against it; that night the garrison blew up the fort\'s magazine and slipped away. The dead were buried in a mass grave on the site. The ground later passed through a mining company and private hands, became a state historic site in 1968 and was listed on the National Register in 1970.',
  'The earthen walls and moat of the fort are still to be seen, with the crater the magazine left when it was blown up. A monument marks the mass grave. The visitor centre has a museum with a fibre-optic diorama of the battle. Sources give the walls as 5 or 9 feet high, and 100 or 150 feet long.',
  'A fort its own garrison blew up and walked away from in the night.',
  'Battle of Pilot Knob State Historic Site (Missouri State Parks), 118 East Maple, Pilot Knob. Parking at the day-use lot off Route 221, north of the fort. Grounds sunrise to sunset all year; the visitor centre gives free tours. Hours from the site\'s own hours page.',
  [W('Fort Davidson'),['Missouri State Parks: Battle of Pilot Knob',MSP+'historic-site/battle-pilot-knob-state-historic-site'],['Site hours',MSP+'hours/926/historic-site/battle-pilot-knob-state-historic-site']],
  th=[37.62095,-90.63902],thn='Day-use lot, Route 221',brick='no',hours='Grounds sunrise to sunset; visitor centre 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. daily April 16 to October 31, Thursday to Sunday in winter',conf='mixed'),
N('bolduc-house-ste-genevieve','Louis Bolduc House','Ste. Genevieve','kcmo','landmark','standing',37.97861,-90.0431,'exact','open','walk','','about 1793–',
  'A French Creole house of upright timbers set on a sill (poteaux sur sole), with a hipped Norman roof, first lived in about 1793 by the well-off Bolduc family in one of the first European settlements in what is now Missouri. It was much altered until the middle of the 20th century, then restored close to its first look and furnished with 18th- and 19th-century household goods. It was the first old building in Ste. Genevieve to be restored and was made a National Historic Landmark in 1970.',
  'The timber house behind its wooden palisade fence, showing how the colonial town lots were laid out, with period furnishings inside. Tours of the house start from the Centre for French Colonial Life at 198 Market Street, where tickets are sold.',
  'An 18th-century French colonial house of upright logs on the Mississippi.',
  'Owned and run by French Colonial America as part of its museum campus in Ste. Genevieve; the town is also part of Ste. Genevieve National Historical Park. Tickets and tours from 198 Market Street; street parking in town (not checked). Hours from French Colonial America\'s own page.',
  [W('Louis Bolduc House'),['NPS: Louis Bolduc House','https://www.nps.gov/places/louis-bolduc-house.htm'],['French Colonial America','https://www.frenchcolonialamerica.org/']],
  brick='no',hours='Museum campus and house tours 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. Monday to Saturday, noon to 4 p.m. Sunday'),
N('lemp-brewery','Lemp Brewery complex','St. Louis','kcmo','industry','standing',38.59111,-90.2175,'site','view','drive','','1865–1919',
  'Adam Lemp, a German immigrant grocer, opened a brewery and saloon in St. Louis in 1840. His son William bought this property in 1863 and the first brewhouse here went up in 1865, over natural caves that were used to lager the beer; the Lemp Mansion was joined to the brewery by a tunnel through the caves. The Griesedieck Beverage Company, later Falstaff, bought the brewery in 1920.',
  'A complex of 27 buildings on 13.7 acres in the Marine Villa neighbourhood, at 3500 Lemp Avenue, used since about 1980 by tenants for light industry, warehouses, offices and artists\' studios. The cellars below were used as a Halloween haunted house in the 1990s. Interior not checked.',
  'A brewing dynasty\'s whole works, still standing over the caves where its beer was lagered.',
  'A private property with working tenants: see it from Lemp Avenue and the streets round it. The sources read describe no public tours of the brewery buildings.',
  [W('Lemp Brewery')],brick='yes'),
N('anderson-house-lexington-mo','Oliver Anderson House and Lexington battlefield','Lexington','kcmo','landmark','standing',39.19094,-93.88066,'exact','open','walk','','1853–',
  'Oliver Anderson, a hemp manufacturer from Kentucky, built this three-storey Greek Revival house in 1853; a local paper called it the largest and best arranged house west of St. Louis. In 1861 the family was turned out and the Union side made it a hospital beside its fortifications. In the three-day Battle of Lexington in September 1861 it changed hands, and Union troops who retook it shot three State Guard prisoners at the foot of the main stair. The house and battlefield were listed on the National Register in 1969.',
  'Holes from cannon and rifle fire are still to be seen in the walls inside and outside the house, which is shown by tour. The battlefield round it has a short loop trail and a visitor centre.',
  'A mansion still holed by the cannon of an 1861 siege.',
  'Battle of Lexington State Historic Site (Missouri State Parks), 1101 Delaware Street, Lexington. Parking on Delaware Street and Wood Street. Grounds sunrise to sunset daily. From November to March tours are by appointment. Hours from the site\'s own hours page.',
  [W('Battle of Lexington State Historic Site'),W('First Battle of Lexington'),['Missouri State Parks: the Oliver Anderson House',MSP+'key-location/oliver-anderson-house'],['Site hours',MSP+'hours/806/historic-site/battle-lexington-state-historic-site']],
  th=[39.18978,-93.88016],thn='Delaware Street lot',brick='yes',hours='House tours 10 a.m., noon and 2 p.m., Tuesday to Sunday April to July, Wednesday to Sunday August to October; winter by appointment'),
N('missouri-mines-federal-mill-3','Federal Mill No. 3 (Missouri Mines)','Park Hills','kcmo','industry','standing',37.83833,-90.50917,'site','open','walk','','1906–1972',
  'The Federal Lead Company built this mill in 1906 and 1907 to process the lead and zinc ore mined round it in the Old Lead Belt. The St. Joseph Lead Company bought it in 1923 and ran it until 1972, when the ore was largely worked out and mining moved west. The land was given to the state in 1975; the scarred ground round it became St. Joe State Park and the mill an interpretive site.',
  'The big mill buildings and conveyors of the processing plant. The former powerhouse is a museum of the area\'s lead mining with machinery from the mines and a large mineral collection.',
  'A whole lead-ore mill left standing in the Old Lead Belt.',
  'Missouri Mines State Historic Site (Missouri State Parks), 4000 State Highway 32, Park Hills. The museum charges a fee (the site has a fees page). Parking off the paved bicycle trail south of the mill. Hours from the site\'s own hours page.',
  [W('Missouri Mines State Historic Site'),['Missouri State Parks: Missouri Mines',MSP+'historic-site/missouri-mines-state-historic-site'],['Site hours',MSP+'hours/786/historic-site/missouri-mines-state-historic-site']],
  th=[37.83665,-90.50872],thn='Lot south of the mill',brick='yes',hours='9 a.m. to 5 p.m. Wednesday to Sunday April to November; Saturday and Sunday December to March'),
N('deutschheim-hermann','Deutschheim: the Pommer-Gentner and Strehly houses','Hermann','kcmo','landmark','standing',38.70625,-91.43799,'site','open','walk','','1840–1890',
  'Houses and outbuildings of German immigrants who settled the Missouri River valley at Hermann in the middle of the 19th century, bought by the state in 1978. The Pommer-Gentner house of 1840 is one of the oldest buildings left in Hermann; the Carl Strehly house was built in 1842. The site keeps a barn, a winery, vines in one of the original vineyards and the site of a printing press.',
  'The two houses, furnished as German-Missouri homes of the period and shown by guided tour, with gardens and exhibits on the immigrants\' journey and their part on the Union side in the Civil War.',
  'Two of the first houses of a town built to be German.',
  'Deutschheim State Historic Site (Missouri State Parks): visitor centre at 101 West Second Street, Hermann. Grounds and gardens sunrise to sunset daily. Street parking in town (not checked). Hours from the site\'s own hours page.',
  [W('Deutschheim State Historic Site'),['Missouri State Parks: Deutschheim',MSP+'historic-site/deutschheim-state-historic-site'],['Site hours',MSP+'hours/796/historic-site/deutschheim-state-historic-site']],
  brick='yes',hours='Tours 10 a.m., 12:30 and 2:30 p.m., daily April to October, Thursday to Sunday November to March'),
N('hunter-dawson-house','Hunter-Dawson House','New Madrid','kcmo','landmark','standing',36.59652,-89.52528,'exact','open','walk','','1859–1860',
  'A 15-room house built in 1859 and 1860 for the merchant William Washington Hunter and his wife Amanda. He died of yellow fever just before building began and she finished it; local craftsmen and at least some of the family\'s enslaved people built it. Her daughter Ella and Ella\'s husband William Dawson inherited it in 1876, and it stayed in the family until 1958. The city bought it in 1966 and gave it to the state.',
  'A Bootheel mansion still holding an estimated 80 per cent of Amanda Hunter\'s own furniture, shown furnished as in the 1860s to 1880s, by guided tour on the hour.',
  'A Bootheel mansion that kept nearly all its first owner\'s furniture.',
  'Hunter-Dawson State Historic Site (Missouri State Parks), 312 Dawson Road, New Madrid. Parking off Route U north-east of the house. Hours from the site\'s own hours page.',
  [W('Hunter-Dawson State Historic Site'),['Missouri State Parks: Hunter-Dawson',MSP+'historic-site/hunter-dawson-state-historic-site'],['Site hours',MSP+'hours/841/historic-site/hunter-dawson-state-historic-site']],
  th=[36.59836,-89.52155],thn='Route U lot',brick='no',hours='Tours on the hour 10 a.m. to 3 p.m. Tuesday to Saturday, noon to 3 p.m. Sunday (Sunday tours April to October only)'),
N('arrow-rock-village','Arrow Rock village and the J. Huston Tavern','Arrow Rock','kcmo','town','standing',39.06991,-92.94508,'site','open','walk','','1834–',
  'A river town above the Missouri River in the Boone\'s Lick country and a stop on the Santa Fe Trail, now a quiet village. Part of it is a state historic site and the whole is a National Historic Landmark district. The J. Huston Tavern dates from 1834 and is still a restaurant; the painter George Caleb Bingham built a house here.',
  'Streets of old buildings: the 1834 tavern, with its old kitchen and upstairs bedrooms shown furnished, the old courthouse, the town doctor\'s house, a stone jail and the Bingham house, furnished as in the 1880s. A visitor centre has exhibits on the town. The site\'s 1930s Works Progress Administration bridge and shelters are on the National Register too.',
  'A Santa Fe Trail river town that stopped growing and so kept itself.',
  'Arrow Rock State Historic Site (Missouri State Parks) and the Village of Arrow Rock. Grounds 7 a.m. to 10 p.m. daily. The tavern keeps restaurant hours of its own (not checked). Hours from the site\'s own hours page.',
  [W('Arrow Rock State Historic Site'),['Missouri State Parks: Arrow Rock',MSP+'historic-site/arrow-rock-state-historic-site'],['Site hours',MSP+'hours/906/historic-site/arrow-rock-state-historic-site']],
  brick='yes',hours='Visitor centre 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. daily March to November, Friday to Sunday December to February'),
N('mark-twain-boyhood-home','Mark Twain Boyhood Home','Hannibal','kcmo','landmark','standing',39.71205,-91.3579,'exact','open','walk','','1844–',
  'Samuel Clemens, who wrote as Mark Twain, lived in this house on Hill Street from 1844 to 1853 and drew many of his stories from those years. It has been open as a museum since 1912 and was made a National Historic Landmark in 1962. It is one of nine properties of the museum, which include the house of Laura Hawkins, his childhood sweetheart, the office of his father as justice of the peace, and Grant\'s Drug Store.',
  'The boyhood house behind the whitewashed fence of the Tom Sawyer story, in the Mark Twain Historic District, with the museum\'s other buildings round it. The Becky Thatcher house had its outside restored in 2010 and reopened inside in 2016.',
  'The house and fence behind Tom Sawyer.',
  'Mark Twain Boyhood Home & Museum, a private museum, 206 to 208 Hill Street, Hannibal; gallery at 120 North Main Street. Its own website refused to be read when this was written, so hours and fees are not checked. Street parking in town (not checked).',
  [W('Mark Twain Boyhood Home & Museum'),['Mark Twain Boyhood Home & Museum','https://marktwainmuseum.org/']],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('pony-express-stables','Pony Express stables (Pony Express National Museum)','St. Joseph','kcmo','landmark','standing',39.75613,-94.84857,'exact','open','walk','','1858–',
  'Built in 1858 as the Pike\'s Peak Stables, a wooden barn for the horses of a freight and stagecoach company, this is where westbound Pony Express riders set out from 1860 to 1861. Its outer walls were rebuilt in brick in 1888, and it is not certain how much of the first frame was kept or reused. It now holds the Pony Express National Museum.',
  'A one-storey stable whose front and side walls are coursed brick, two bricks thick; the back wall was wood on a brick base in 1970. Inside are dioramas, maps, artifacts and an archaeological dig.',
  'The barn the first Pony Express rider rode out of.',
  'Pony Express National Museum, a private museum, 914 Penn Street, St. Joseph. Admission $10 for adults when this was written. Closed on major holidays. Hours and fees from the museum\'s own page.',
  [W('Pony Express Museum'),['Pony Express National Museum: hours and admission','https://www.ponyexpress.org/hours-and-admission']],
  brick='yes',hours='9 a.m. to 5 p.m. Monday to Saturday, 11 a.m. to 4 p.m. Sunday'),
N('ray-house-wilsons-creek','Ray House, Wilson\'s Creek battlefield','Republic','kcmo','landmark','standing',37.10682,-93.39811,'exact','public','drive','','about 1852–',
  'A farmhouse finished about 1852 by John Ray, a Union-leaning farmer and postmaster. On August 10, 1861, in the Battle of Wilson\'s Creek, the first major Civil War battle west of the Mississippi, he watched fighting in his own cornfield from the porch while his family, an enslaved woman named Rhoda, her children and a hired hand hid in the cellar. The house became a Southern field hospital under a yellow flag, and the body of Gen. Nathaniel Lyon, the first Union general killed in combat in the war, was laid on the family\'s bed.',
  'The restored farmhouse at stop 2 of the battlefield tour road, with the cornfield and Bloody Hill beyond. The battlefield has changed little except for trees and brush. The bed and the bedspread that covered Lyon are in the park\'s museum collection. Interior opening not checked.',
  'The farmhouse that became a field hospital, with a general laid on its bed.',
  'Wilson\'s Creek National Battlefield (National Park Service), visitor centre at 5242 South State Highway ZZ, Republic. Parking at stop 2 of the tour road. The park\'s hours page is drawn by script and could not be read when this was written: hours and fees not checked.',
  [W('Wilson\'s Creek National Battlefield'),['NPS: Ray House','https://www.nps.gov/places/ray-house.htm']],
  th=[37.10809,-93.39835],thn='Tour road stop 2, Ray Farm',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('sandy-creek-covered-bridge','Sandy Creek Covered Bridge','Hillsboro','kcmo','landmark','standing',38.29399,-90.52669,'exact','public','walk','','1872–',
  'One of six bridges built in 1872 on the Old Lemay Ferry Road to join the county seat at Hillsboro to St. Louis County; John H. Morse built it for $2,000. High water destroyed it in 1886 and Henry Steffin rebuilt it for $899, reusing half the first timbers and the old abutments. It became a state historic site in 1967 and was restored in 1984. It is one of Missouri\'s four surviving covered bridges, which once numbered about 30.',
  'A red Howe-truss covered bridge, 74.5 feet long, over Sandy Creek, open to people on foot, with picnic tables and an interpretive display.',
  'A red barn of a bridge on an old road to St. Louis.',
  'Sandy Creek Covered Bridge State Historic Site (Missouri State Parks), 9090 Old Lemay Ferry Road, north of Hillsboro. Grounds 8 a.m. to half an hour after sunset daily. Hours from the site\'s own hours page.',
  [W('Sandy Creek Covered Bridge State Historic Site'),['Missouri State Parks: Sandy Creek Covered Bridge',MSP+'historic-site/sandy-creek-covered-bridge-state-historic-site'],['Site hours',MSP+'hours/941/historic-site/sandy-creek-covered-bridge-state-historic-site']],
  th=[38.29495,-90.52545],thn='Old Lemay Ferry Road lot',brick='no',hours='Grounds 8 a.m. to half an hour after sunset, daily'),
]
